<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Josiah</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">reigned c. 640–<a href="/years/609-bce">609 BCE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century-bce">7th century BCE</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-david">House of David</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Josiah was king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judah">Judah</a> from about 640 to 609 BCE. According to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bible">Bible</a>, the discovery of a 'book of the law' in the Temple during his reign led him to carry out a sweeping religious reform, abolishing other cults and centralising worship in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>. He was killed at Megiddo in 609 BCE in an encounter with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/necho-ii">Pharaoh Necho II</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/josiah.jpg" alt="Josiah" width="320" height="471" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: This image was produced by me, David Castor (dcastor). The pictures I submit to the Wikipedia Project are released to the public domain. … · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%C3%85hus_kyrka-12.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>According to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew-bible">Hebrew Bible</a>, Josiah was the 16th king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judah">Judah</a>. He became king at the age of eight after his father Amon was assassinated, and he reigned 31 years. His grandfather Manasseh, who turned the Temple to idolatrous worship, was among the kings blamed for turning away from the worship of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yhwh">YHWH</a>, and his great-grandfather <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hezekiah">Hezekiah</a> was a noted reformer. Josiah's reign is difficult to reconstruct. No contemporary text from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a> mentions him, but a seal bearing the name Nathan-melech, an official under Josiah named in 2 Kings, was found in place at a site in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>. Judah remained largely under foreign suzerainty during his reign.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>When Josiah came to the throne around 641 or 640 BCE the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyrian Empire</a> was beginning to break up and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a> had not yet risen, so <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> could govern itself without outside interference. In his 18th year, according to both biblical accounts, he ordered repairs to the Temple. While clearing the treasure room the high priest Hilkiah is said to have found a scroll called the Book of the Law. The prophetess Huldah told the king that the evils foretold in it for disobedience would come, but not in his day.</p><p>Josiah then encouraged the exclusive worship of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yhwh">YHWH</a>. Objects of the worship of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baal">Baal</a> were removed from the Temple, local sanctuaries from Beersheba to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bethel">Bethel</a> were destroyed, and priests of other cults were executed. He also revived the celebration of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/passover">Passover</a>. Scholars hold that the book was either a copy of Deuteronomy or a text that became part of it. Some reject the historicity of these accounts entirely, while others defend a reform under Josiah.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In the spring of 609 BCE Pharaoh Necho II led an army north to aid the collapsing Assyrian empire against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medes">Medes</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonians">Babylonians</a>. Josiah led the Judean army to block his way at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-megiddo">Megiddo</a> and was killed. Why he did so is not known. The account in 2 Kings says only that Necho met and killed him, while 2 Chronicles says he was wounded by Egyptian archers and died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>. Some scholars consider the Chronicles account unreliable, because it resembles the death of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahab">Ahab</a>.</p><p>His son Jehoahaz succeeded him but reigned only three months before Necho deposed him and took him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, replacing him with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jehoiakim">Jehoiakim</a> and imposing a levy of a hundred <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/talent">talents</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> and a talent of gold. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deuteronomistic-history">Deuteronomistic History</a>, which many scholars see as first written in his time, holds Josiah the one king to fully carry out the book's laws.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Josiah” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Hezekiah is credited with a first reform, and <b>Josiah</b> (c. 640–609 BCE), after a book of the law was found in the temple in 622 BCE, centralised worship in Jerusalem, destroyed local shrines and celebrated a great Passover.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-judah">Kingdom of Judah · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>…at first one of several cults in the land, and many scholars think the worship of one God alone, monotheism, took shape gradually, through the prophets who denounced the worship of other gods and through the reforms of King <b>Josiah</b> of Judah in the late 7th century BCE.</blockquote><a href="/religion/judaism">Judaism · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Josiah</b>, son of chief Arapsang, explained the picture of Rodgers as part animal by the colonial government's mistreatment of the Kipsigis, who came to see its people as &quot;man-eaters&quot;.</blockquote><a href="/songs/chemirocha">Chemirocha</a></li><li><blockquote>William <b>Josiah</b> Irons made an English version in 1849 that keeps the rhyme and metre; edited, it is approved for funerals in the Catholic ordinariates for former Anglicans.</blockquote><a href="/songs/dies-irae">Dies irae</a></li><li><blockquote>Necho II began a canal from the Nile to the Red Sea, sent a Phoenician fleet around Africa and killed King <b>Josiah</b> of Judah at Megiddo, but lost to Babylon at Carchemish in 605 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/state/twenty-sixth-dynasty-of-egypt">Twenty-sixth Dynasty of Egypt</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Jerusalem, in what is now <a href="/countries/palestine">Palestine</a>. Died in Jerusalem, in what is now <a href="/countries/palestine">Palestine</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=31.78,35.23&amp;year=-620" aria-label="Jerusalem on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5458 1007 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Josiah"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5908 1277h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5908 1277h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=31.78,35.23&amp;year=-620">What was here in 620 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;…great is the wrath of the LORD that is kindled against us, because our fathers have not hearkened unto the words of this book…&#8221;</p><footer>2 Kings 22:13, when the book of the law was found in the Temple &#183; King James Version, 1611 &#183; as the Second Book of Kings tells it &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Bible_(King_James)/2_Kings" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 595 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
